# The Fundraiser Who Never Made a Cold Call

Frances Weegman has been the alumni relations coordinator at a small Lutheran college on a bluff above the Turkey River in northeastern Iowa since a warm Monday in the September of 1991. She is fifty-eight. She has, in the thirty-four Septembers since, never once made a cold call.

She has closed, by the last count her one-person office made on a small yellow legal pad in the June of 2026, forty-one planned gifts. Thirteen of them were seven figures. One of them was the eight-hundred-and-fifty-thousand-dollar gift a retired veterinarian named Dr. Warren H. Thorson named in a folded Christmas card, in the August of 2024, in the phrase *the paper you kept.*

The paper Frances kept was Warren's 1972 senior thesis on the ethics of veterinary anesthesia in dairy herds. It has been in a small green Bankers Box on the second shelf above her desk for fifty-four years. She did not put it there. Her predecessor Marjorie did, on a Friday afternoon in the June of 1972, four days after the commencement ceremony in the small stone chapel on the north end of the quadrangle.

Frances inherited the box, unopened, in the September of 1991. She opened it on a rainy Wednesday in the October of that year. She has written to Warren, on the first Tuesday of every month, since a Tuesday in the November of 1991.

## The industry we built for the other fundraiser

Sometime in the November of 1998, the sector's largest conference — held that year in a hotel across the parking lot from the Atlanta airport — turned decisively toward the sales floor.

The keynote was given by a man in a navy blazer with a small brass pin from a Fortune 500 software company. He had, twelve years earlier, run enterprise sales for a database vendor whose product the sector had never heard of. He said the word *pipeline* fourteen times in forty-two minutes. He said the phrase *moves management* nine times. He did not, in any of the forty-two minutes, say the word *letter.*

For the twenty-eight years since, the sector has been re-tooling around his slides. The software the industry has bought — the CRMs, the wealth screens, the portfolio dashboards, the sequence engines, the AI-powered "next-best-action" widgets — has all been built on one presupposition, imported unexamined from the enterprise sales floor of the late nineteen-nineties.

The presupposition is: *the buyer does not know the seller.*

The whole apparatus of major-gifts training — cold calls, discovery visits, capacity bands, MEDDIC-for-nonprofits, moves management, the twenty-page qualification checklist — is designed for a stranger. It has to be. It was borrowed from a sales floor whose entire job was to convert strangers into customers by Friday.

The donor is not a stranger.

The donor has been reading the alumni magazine for forty-two years. The donor knows, better than three of your trustees, the name of the associate dean who retired in the spring of 2004. The donor has been quietly waiting, since a Wednesday in the November of 1991, for a letter written in the tone of a friend rather than a sales rep.

Frances writes in that tone. She has been doing it since Warren was thirty-nine.

## What Frances actually does on a Tuesday morning

At seven-forty-one on the first Tuesday of every month, Frances opens the small navy quadrille notebook and writes one page, in her own hand, to Warren. The letter is never about money.

It is, in the July of 2023, about a small paragraph on page fourteen of the summer alumni magazine about a Rhode Island Red rooster the biology department kept in a pen behind the greenhouse from 1969 to 1974, which Warren had, in a Christmas card in the December of 2011, mentioned by name (his name was Elmer). It is, in the November of 2019, about a black-and-white photograph the college archives had just uploaded, of a small chapel choir in a snowy December of 1971 in which Warren, at nineteen, sang second tenor two rows back on the left.

She signs each letter *fondly, Frances,* in blue ballpoint, and mails it Wednesday morning from the small post office on Main Street in Elkader on her way to a Wednesday-morning book club.

Warren has written back to every letter but three since a warm Wednesday in the April of 1992. He has, in the thirty-four years, mentioned the college's endowment exactly once, in a folded Christmas card in the August of 2024.

That was the phrase *the paper you kept.*

## The tool the sector needs is not the tool the sector bought

The software the sector has bought, for twenty-five years, cannot help Frances. It cannot hurt her either — she does not open it. She has, in the thirty-four years, run exactly zero wealth screens on Warren. She has, in the thirty-four years, run exactly zero next-best-action queries against the pipeline. She has, in the thirty-four years, filed exactly zero moves-management notes on Warren's contact record. Warren's contact record, on the fourth screen of the shop's donor CRM, reads *no engagement, mail only, do not upgrade.*

That is what the tool was built to say about Warren.

The reason it says it is that the tool was built for the other fundraiser. It was built for the officer in a hundred-person shop at a medium-sized private research university on the East Coast, whose calendar the vice president of advancement fills, on the third Sunday of every August, with the twenty names the wealth screen returned that quarter. That officer's job — as her employer defines it — is to convert twenty strangers into meetings by December.

That officer has a real job. The tool helps her. Fine.

But the tool has, in trying to sell to her employer, quietly redefined the whole sector's idea of what a fundraiser is. It has told a generation of small-shop directors that Frances's practice is quaint. That her spreadsheet is embarrassing. That her notebook is a *data hygiene issue.* That her thirty-four years of Tuesday-morning letters are, in the language of the industry's white papers, *unstructured engagement without measurable pipeline impact.*

Forty-one planned gifts. Thirteen seven figures. One in the phrase *the paper you kept.*

## What Rōmy is for

Rōmy is not designed to help Frances make a cold call. Frances does not make cold calls. Rōmy is designed to help her be more of what she already is: a good listener with a small navy quadrille notebook and a Wednesday-morning trip to the post office in Elkader.

It reads, before she gets to her desk on Tuesday morning, the small careful pages the fifty-eighth alumni magazine of 2026 quietly ran on page forty-six, the *Turkey Valley Times* birthday column for the second week of August, the Rotary Club installation photograph from the *Decorah Public Opinion* in the June of 1979, and the small paragraph in the 1972 yearbook where Warren, at twenty-two, in the section reserved for graduating seniors' one-line thanks to a professor, wrote *Dr. J. R. Nordmeyer — for teaching me that a good vet is a good listener first.*

Then it puts, on Frances's desk at seven-thirty-nine, a small careful page a good listener would have written for her, if the good listener had thirty hours in a Tuesday morning instead of eight.

The letter is still Frances's. The signature is still hers. The trip to the post office in Elkader is still hers.

The tool is smaller than the sector has been selling for twenty-five years.

That is the whole point.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Tuesday morning

Pull one file, this week, out of your CRM whose engagement notes are blank.

Not because the donor is disengaged. Because Frances never worked there.

Read her, for twenty minutes. Her last Christmas card, if there is one. The small paragraph in the alumni magazine class notes of the fall of 2014 that mentioned her mother's ninetieth birthday in a small town in Nebraska. The plain fact of the address the file has had, unchanged, since a Saturday in the June of 1978.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write one page in your own hand. Do not ask her anything. Tell her one small true thing you noticed. Sign it *fondly.*

Mail it Wednesday morning.

She has been waiting since 1991. ♡
